23:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-16","children":[["$","section","01-two-networks",{"id":"01-two-networks","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"01"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Two networks, one router"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Our setup: Network A is 192.168.1.0/24 with hosts A1 (.2), A2 (.3), A3 (.4). Network B is 192.168.2.0/24 with hosts B1 (.2), B2 (.3), B3 (.4). Both use the mask 255.255.255.0. A single router sits between them and holds an IP in each network."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L25",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch06%2F01-two-networks.jpg?v=374ca5aea1a79f8c832b9ef5f66dc1a9","alt":"Network A (192.168.1.0/24) and Network B (192.168.2.0/24) joined by a router with two interfaces."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}],["$","section","02-router-interfaces",{"id":"02-router-interfaces","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"02"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"A router has many interfaces"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The important insight: a router (default gateway) has multiple network interfaces, each with its own IP on a different network. One interface might face your home network, another face your ISP (Jio, Airtel, etc.). This is what lets a router connect separate networks."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L25",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch06%2F02-router-interfaces.jpg?v=40c290794db1cf07c070bdb118a83d33","alt":"A router with two ports, one facing the home network, one facing the ISP."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}],["$","section","03-intra-calc",{"id":"03-intra-calc","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"A1 → A3: run the mask"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A1 wants to reach A3. It ANDs its own IP (192.168.1.2) with the mask → 192.168.1.0, and ANDs A3’s IP (192.168.1.4) with the mask → 192.168.1.0. The last octet is zeroed out in both. The results match, same network."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L25",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch06%2F03-intra-calc.jpg?v=e63251a13ac8935ddd0422b34f73e635","alt":"A1 and A3 both reduce to 192.168.1.0, same network."}],"$L26"]}]]}]]}],"$L27","$L28","$L29","$L2a"]}]
